WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:03.320
Max Fisher, Vox.com Foreign Editor: Russia
is invading Europe in 2014.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:04.580
This is a big deal.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:08.170
CNN Anchor: "The Russian military now being
directly involved in fighting Ukrainian government

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:09.170
forces."

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:14.990
Samantha Power: "Satellite imagery show Russian
combat units -- combat units southeast of

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:16.640
Donetsk in Eastern Ukraine."

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:20.420
So President Obama, western leaders, everybody
is furious about this.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:25.170
Obama: "Russia has deliberately and repeatedly
violated the sovereignty and territorial integrity

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.170
of Ukraine."

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:29.930
To understand why this all happening in Ukraine,
you have to go actually have to go back to

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:33.750
2012, which is when Russian President Vladimir
Putin ran for re-election.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.090
There were big protests in Moscow, St. Petersburg,
and a couple other places.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:41.780
He completely panicked, and that's when he
shifted political strategies from economic

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:49.590
growth to anti-western paranoia, conservative
Christian orthodoxy, and a Russian imperial

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.840
revival of nationalism.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:55.901
Then jump ahead to November 2013, that's when
the Ukraine crisis breaks out.

00:00:55.901 --> 00:01:00.180
You have protesters in Kiev that want to topple
the pro-Russian government and replace it

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:01.539
with a pro-European government.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:05.580
Putin sees this as a direct threat, fomented
by the Americans against Russia.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:10.030
When the government falls, that's when he
invades to annex Crimea.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:14.780
He gains a huge popularity boost in Russia
over this, it works out for him wonderfully

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:15.780
politically.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.270
And he gets addicted to it.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:22.310
And that's why shortly after that, he starts
supporting these pro-Russia separatist rebels

00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:23.479
in Eastern Ukraine.

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:27.530
The rebels take over a couple of cities, Putin
starts escalating, and in July he hands the

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.800
rebels these very sophisticated surface-to-air
missiles which they use to shoot down Malaysian

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:32.930
Airlines flight 17.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:36.440
CNN anchor: "US officials say pro-Russian
rebels are responsible"

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:41.140
Ukraine is fed up, the west is fed up, Ukrainian
military moves in to re-take these eastern

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.899
cities, and actually push out a number of
these rebels.

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:48.270
Putin really panics because he thinks he's
going to lose everything and has bared this

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:50.799
huge cost of economic sanctions for doing
it.

00:01:50.799 --> 00:01:56.020
He couldn't afford to build up the crisis
as so crucial for Russia and then just kind

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:57.399
of watch these rebels lose.

00:01:57.399 --> 00:02:01.900
So he was pulled into an invasion that clearly
is not going to work out in his long term

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:02.900
strategic interests.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:07.259
And the thing that makes that so scary is
that if that's the case, that means he has

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:12.160
no strategy, it means he has no actual objective
that he's trying to achieve, and it means

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.360
that there's no clear end in sight for this
crisis.

